### 3. Additional context (if relevant):                                                                                
- Borderline definitions: The protocol emphasizes mHealth-delivered education (apps, SMS, web on mobile, social media,  
other mobile/wireless). Telephone-delivered health coaching is a mobile/wireless modality; whether it qualifies may     
depend on strictness. I included study 67 and (conservatively) 65 for full-text confirmation of explicit educational    
content.                                                                                                                
- Frequent exclusions: Many studies were telemonitoring-only, device remote monitoring, provider-focused interventions  
(e-mail reminders to nurses), economic models, systematic reviews, qualitative or quasi-experimental designs, or non-HF 
populations. Several educational interventions used non-mobile media (CD-ROM, in-person/Photo-CD), which fall outside   
mHealth per protocol.                                                                                                   
- Recommended full-text checks:                                                                                         
  - Study 65: Verify the intervention details to confirm structured educational counseling via telephone beyond         
monitoring.                                                                                                             
  - Study 67: Confirm that health coaching content is educational/self-care–oriented and delivered primarily via phone  
(meets criteria).                                                                                                       
- Confidence: Medium overall, driven by ambiguity in whether telephone-based coaching is considered mHealth education   
per the protocol; low uncertainty for exclusions that are clearly non-RCT, non-HF, or non-mobile education.             
